Architecture 

The following diagram is an overview of the current architecture of the ETL pipeline used by CRMA HIVE.  

 

Components 

Amazon S3:  

Raw Data Storage - Stores ingested raw data from external sources for initial processing. 

Processed Data Storage - Holds cleaned, quarantined, as well as data with warnings  

Lifecycle Policy - Archives raw and processed data into AWS S3 Glacier for long-term historical data retention purposes 

AWS Secrets Manager – Stores credentials for external APIs, S3 buckets, and the target RDS database, ensuring sensitive information is protected and accessible only by authorized services. 

AWS Glue Jobs - Ingest raw data, process and classify, load processed data to target database, and extract, parse and normalize records to generate records for derived datasets. 

AWS RDS – Contains the crma_hive schema, tables, and views used to store the processed, quarantined, and warnings datasets 

AWS CloudWatch - Aggregates application logs from glue jobs. Supports debugging and troubleshooting efforts 

AWS EventBridge/SNS – Monitors and alert dev team on pipeline success and failures. 

Infrastructure Overview 

The ETL pipeline is deployed in the AWS cloud environment, leveraging managed services for scalability and reliability. Infrastructure provisioning is automated using Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform, ensuring consistent and repetable deployments. For source code managment and version control, the team uses GitHub, which enables collaboration and semless update to the pipeline. Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) are managed via Jenkins, orchestrating a structed deployment process. This process includes running unit tests to verify the correctness of Glue ETL scripts, scanning the infrastructure code to identify potential security risks, and finally deploying resources to the AWS environment. For a detailed list of AWS resources deployed view HIVE - AWS Infrastructure.xlsx 

 

